<h2>How the reference arose</h2>
<p>The reference came from the trial floor. The ASJ-06 (POCSO) at Saket, seized of Sessions Case 741/2025 arising from FIR No. 479/2021, Police Station Lajpat Nagar, confronted a forum question created by the Juvenile Justice (Amendment) Act, 2021: once Parliament directed that certain offences against children under the JJ Act be tried by the Children&rsquo;s Court, what happens to trials of those offences already pending before Judicial Magistrates — and does the special child-protection framework pull in every offence with a minor victim? Rather than guess, the judge referred the questions to the High Court under Section 436 BNSS, the provision that allows a Sessions Court to refer questions of law. The Division Bench appointed an amicus curiae and answered both.</p>

<h2>The two answers</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Question 1 — retrospectivity.</strong> Section 86(4) of the JJ Act, as amended with effect from 1 September 2022, applies retrospectively. Pending trials of covered offences before Judicial Magistrates First Class stand transferred to the Children&rsquo;s Court.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Question 2 — scope.</strong> General offences under the BNS or IPC where the victim is a minor are not exclusively triable by the Children&rsquo;s Court under Section 25 of the Commissions for Protection of Child Rights Act, 2005 — unless another statute specifically provides for that forum.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why retrospectivity follows from principle</h2>
<p>Forum and procedure stand on a different constitutional footing from substantive liability. No litigant has a vested right to be tried by a particular court; a change in forum is a change in procedure, and procedural law ordinarily applies to pending proceedings. The amendment to Section 86 reflected a legislative judgment that offences against children under the JJ Act deserve the seniority, specialisation and sentencing powers of the Children&rsquo;s Court. Leaving pre-amendment cases to be completed before magistrates would have produced two classes of child victims distinguished only by the accident of filing dates. The retrospective reading avoids that incongruity while taking nothing of substance from the accused, whose defences, burdens and punishments remain as they were.</p>

<h2>The equally important limit</h2>
<p>The second answer is the brake on over-reading the first. Section 25 of the CPCR Act permits designation of courts for speedy trial of offences against children, and it is sometimes argued that every prosecution with a minor victim — a theft from a child, an assault on one — must therefore travel to the designated court. The Bench declined that view. Exclusive jurisdiction is a creature of express statutory allocation: POCSO cases go to Special Courts because the POCSO Act says so; JJ Act offences go to the Children&rsquo;s Court because Section 86 now says so. A general BNS offence with a minor victim follows the ordinary forum rules of the BNSS unless some statute directs otherwise. The result is a clean two-rail system rather than a jurisdictional land-grab.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case type</th><th>Forum after this ruling</th></tr>
  <tr><td>JJ Act offences against children (covered by amended S.86)</td><td>Children&rsquo;s Court — including trials pending before magistrates, which transfer</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POCSO Act offences</td><td>Special Court under POCSO, as before</td></tr>
  <tr><td>General BNS/IPC offences with a minor victim</td><td>Ordinary competent court under the BNSS, unless a statute specifically allocates the case elsewhere</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What litigants should do now</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Audit pending files.</strong> Prosecutions under the JJ Act pending before magistrates in Delhi should be checked against amended Section 86 and moved for transfer where covered.</p>
  <p><strong>Resist forum inflation.</strong> Defence counsel facing an argument that any minor-victim case belongs to the Children&rsquo;s Court now have a Division Bench answer to the contrary.</p>
  <p><strong>Watch the transition.</strong> Transfers mid-trial raise practical questions — stage of evidence, de novo concerns, bail continuity — which the transferee court will manage under the BNSS&rsquo;s general provisions.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a Division Bench judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
